The Queue interface in Java, part of the java.util package, (mainly for processing the element, not for storing)
  represents an ordered collection designed for holding elements prior to processing,
  typically following the First-In-First-Out (FIFO) principle.
  Elements are added to the rear (tail) and removed from the front (head)

FIFO Ordering:
   Elements are processed in the order they were added, similar to a real-world waiting line.
   The PriorityQueue implementation is an exception, as it orders elements based on a specified priority.
No Index Access:
   Elements cannot be accessed by an index; access is limited to operations at the head and tail
Implementations:
   Since Queue is an interface, it cannot be instantiated directly. You must use a concrete class that implements it, such as
  LinkedList: A basic, general-purpose FIFO queue implementation using a doubly linked list internally.
  PriorityQueue: Orders elements based on their natural ordering or a custom Comparator, not strictly FIFO.
  ArrayDeque: Implements the Deque (Double-Ended Queue) interface, which extends Queue. It's efficient for insertions and deletions at both ends.
  BlockingQueue implementations: Found in the java.util.concurrent package,
     these are thread-safe and support operations that wait for the queue to become non-empty or for space to become available


Real life scenarios:

  -> Tasks → Queue → Worker Threads

  -> ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);
     ExecutorService internally uses queue, So tasks are processed one by one in order.
  -> Producer–Consumer Problem  -  Producer → Queue → Consumer
  -> Event Handling  -  Events → Queue → Event Handlers
  -> Message Queues  -  Messages → Queue → Message Processors(Kafka, RabbitMQ)
  -> BFS Algorithm (Graph / Tree) use queue

Normal Queue Operations: (FIFO) -> ArrayDeque, LinkedList
priority queue operations: (Priority) -> PriorityQueue  - VIP → First, Normal → Later
Blocking queue operations: (Thread-Safe) -> LinkedBlockingQueue, ArrayBlockingQueue

| Queue Type    | Storage |
| ------------- | ------- |
| ArrayDeque    | Array   |
| LinkedList    | Nodes   |
| PriorityQueue | Heap    |

Operation Throws       Returns Special    Description
          Exception	   Value
Insert	  add(E e)	   offer(E e)	       Adds an element to the rear of the queue. offer() is generally preferable for capacity-constrained queues.
Remove	  remove()	   poll()	           Retrieves and removes the head of the queue. poll() returns null if the queue is empty, while remove() throws a NoSuchElementException.
Examine	  element()	   peek()	           Retrieves, but does not remove, the head of the queue. peek() returns null if the queue is empty, while element() throws a NoSuchElementException